    "answer": {
      "short": "$k\\leq\\dfrac ac$. A horizontal line at or below the horizontal asymptote selects one interval.",
      "worked": "Assume $c\\ne0$ and $ad-bc\\ne0$. The rational graph has vertical asymptote $x=-\\dfrac dc$ and horizontal asymptote $y=\\dfrac ac$.\n\nAt $k=\\dfrac ac$, one branch lies below the horizontal asymptote and the other above it. The selected branch gives one half-line, excluding the vertical asymptote.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}-\\frac ac&=\\frac{bc-ad}{c(cx+d)}\\\\\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}-\\frac ac&=\\frac{bc-ad}{c^2\\left(x+\\frac dc\\right)}\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $bc-ad>0$, the solution is $x<-\\dfrac dc$. If $bc-ad<0$, the solution is $x>-\\dfrac dc$.\n\nA horizontal line below $y=\\dfrac ac$ also selects one interval, bounded by its intersection with the lower branch and the excluded pole. A line above the asymptote selects two separated intervals. Thus, under these assumptions, all suitable values are $k\\leq\\dfrac ac$; the answer key gives the particular value $k=\\dfrac ac$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
